<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kitab al-I'tibar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1183</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Book of Contemplation, Memoirs of Usama ibn Munqidh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kitab al-I'tibar, or Book of Contemplation, is the memoir of the Syrian Arab nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usama-ibn-munqidh">Usama ibn Munqidh</a>, written in his old age around 1183. Its anecdotes of hunting, warfare, medicine and daily life, including vivid descriptions of the customs of the Frankish crusaders, make it one of the most valuable Muslim sources on the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The book belongs to adab, a literary genre meant to please and divert its readers as well as to instruct them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usama-ibn-munqidh">Usama ibn Munqidh</a>, a diplomat, soldier, hunter and poet, tells of a life that took him, after trouble with his uncle, the ruler of Shayzar, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in the army of the Atabeg Zengi, at the Fatimid court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and in the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>. A recurring theme is that victory in warfare comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> and not from planning or the number of troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Usama describes the possessions of the wealthy: the mansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> assigned to him when he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1144, gifts of Dabiqi linen and Dimyati brocade, and the jewels, weapons and about thirty thousand dinars taken from his family by the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on their journey back from Egypt. His deepest loss was his 4,000 books. He also gives a close view of the plots of the Fatimid court, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Zafir planned the death of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">al-Adil</a>, Nasr ibn Abbas killed al-Adil, and Nasr and his father then killed the caliph.</p><p>Usama calls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> animals possessing the virtues of courage and fighting, but nothing else. He mocks their trials by duel and by water, refuses a knight's offer to take his son to the lands of the Franks to be educated, and repeats a story of a Frankish physician whose amputation killed a knight, though he also records Frankish cures that worked, among them a remedy for scrofula that he used himself. He notes that Franks long settled in the east were less rude than newcomers, and he counted one pilgrim knight as a friend who called him brother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The only known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was found in 1880 in the library of the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">San Lorenzo de El Escorial</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. It has 134 folios, with the first 21 pages missing, and is considered a copy of a copy of the original. Hartwig Derenbourg first mentioned it in Les manuscrits arabes de l'Escurial and studied, transcribed and published the text in Ousama ibn Mounkidh, un émir syrien (1889). Philip K. Hitti's translation was last published by Columbia University Press in 2000; Hitti judged the work superior to other Arabic biographies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kitab al-I'tibar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Usama ibn Munqidh, born there in 1095, wrote the <b>Kitab al-I'tibar</b>, vivid memoirs of hunting, war and Frankish customs.</blockquote><a href="/state/munqidhite-emirate-of-shaizar">Munqidhite Emirate of Shaizar</a></li></ul></section>
